   "editorial_contributions": [
    {
     "language": "English",
     "reviewer": {
      "author_code": "szekely.laszlo-a",
      "reviewer_id": "5891",
      "name": "L\u00e1szl\u00f3 A. Sz\u00e9kely",
      "sign": "L\u00e1szl\u00f3 A. Sz\u00e9kely (Columbia)"
     },
     "text": "For a set of distances \\(D=\\{d_1,d_2,\\dots,d_k\\}\\), a set \\(A\\subset \\mathbb{R}^n\\) is called \\(D\\)-avoiding, if no pair of points of \\(A\\) is at distance \\(d_i\\) for some \\(i\\). The paper shows that the density of \\(A\\) is exponentially small in \\(k\\) provided the ratios \\(d_1/d_2,d_2/d_3,\\dots,d_{k-1}/d_k\\) are all small enough.\n\nThis resolves an old question of the reviewer, and generalizes results of \\textit{H. Furstenberg, Y. Katznelson} and \\textit{B. Weiss} [Coll. Pap. Symp. Graph Theory, Prague/Czech., Algorithms Comb. 5, 184--198 (1990; Zbl 0738.28013)]; \\textit{K. J. Falconer} and \\textit{J. M. Marstrand} [Bull. Lond. Math. Soc. 18, 471--474 (1986; Zbl 0599.28008)], and \\textit{J. Bourgain} [Isr. J. Math. 54, 307--316 (1986; Zbl 0609.10043)].",
     "contribution_type": "review"
    }
   ],
