     "text": "Summary: For a set of distances \\(D=\\{d_{1},\\cdots ,d_k\\}\\) a set \\(A\\) in the plane is called \\(D\\)-avoiding if no pair of points of \\(A\\) is at distance \\(di\\) for some \\(i\\). We show that the density of \\(A\\) is exponentially small in \\(k\\) provided the ratios \\(d_{1}/d_{2},d_{2}/d_{3},\\cdots ,d_{k - 1}/d_k\\) are all small enough. We also show that there exists a largest \\(D\\)-avoiding set, and give an algorithm to compute the maximum density of a \\(D\\)-avoiding set for any \\(D\\).\n\nThe journal version has been published in Geom. Funct. Anal. 18, No. 3, 668--697 (2008; Zbl 1169.52005).\n\nFor the entire collection see [Zbl 1137.05002].",
     "contribution_type": "summary"
    }
   ],
